      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2016 International Society of Blood Transfusion.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "Three leucoreduction filters were evaluated - when used alone or combined with centrifuge leucoreduction (C-LR) - to prevent alloimmune platelet refractoriness in a dog platelet transfusion model."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "Donor platelet-rich plasma (PRP) or buffy coat (BC) platelets were either filter leucoreduced (F-LR) or F-LR/C-LR, (51) Cr radiolabelled and transfused. Weekly transfusions were given for up to 8 weeks or until platelet refractoriness. Recipients who accepted treated transfusions were then given non-leucoreduced (non-LR) platelets to determine whether donor-specific tolerance had been induced."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Acceptance of F-LR PRP transfusions ranged from 29% to 66%. F-LR/C-LR transfusions prepared from PRP were accepted by 92%, from BC by 63% and from pooled PRP by 75% of recipients (p=NS); overall acceptance rate of F-LR/C-LR transfusions was 83%. Tolerance to subsequent non-LR transfusions occurred in 45% of the F-LR-/C-LR-accepting recipients unrelated to DR-B compatibility between donors and recipients (P = 0·18)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "In a dog platelet transfusion model, acceptance of donor platelets required combining F-LR with C-LR as apparently each process removes different immunizing WBCs."
          }
        ]
      },
